We first show that identification of hypercollapsing subterms yields an equivalence relation.

To see this, consider the following four rewrite rules:
\begin{align*}
a & \rightarrow f(b) & b & \rightarrow b\\
a & \rightarrow g(c) & c & \rightarrow c
\end{align*}
\noindent No term in which a redex occurs has a normal form. Hence, NF is immediate. However, confluence modulo hypercollapsing subterms does not hold, as $a$ reduces to $f(b)$ and $g(c)$ --- both of which only reduce to themselves --- and as $f(b) \not \sim_{hc} g(c)$.

